            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house is relatively tight already and may even be at the verge of being slightly under-ventilated. Even so, the access panel in the basement wall (the wood chute) doesn't seal well and allows unnecessary air leakage.



The woodstove is currently drawing combustion air from inside the house, so that air leakage from the wood chute nearby likely helps to reduce smoke from wafting back into the house. Drawing interior air for combustion is a source of heat loss as the conditioned warm air inside is being drawn into the woodstove &amp; sent up the chimney.



The better way is for that Blaze King Princess wood stove to have a dedicated outside combustion air pipe brought in through the upper basement wall and have it directly sealed to the back of the woodstove.



Other sources of air leakage include:



** The co-vented chimney for the furnace and hot water (can be eliminated if you upgrade these 2 appliances to direct vent, high efficiency equipment as outlined within this report)



** The 6" combustion air pipe located between the furnace and hot water tank (can be eliminated if you upgrade these 2 appliances to direct vent, high efficiency equipment as outlined within this report AND provide a dedicated combustion air supply to the woodstove, as outlined above)



Savings: I have modeled a modest 10% reduction in overall air leakage that could potentially save $ 44 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.4 GJ, and qualify for a rebate through the Canada Greener Homes Program.



NOTE: Also read the Ventilation Upgrade section of this report.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the 30% reduction Larger reductions of air leakage can increase this grant up to a maximum $1,300.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <VentilationSystem>

VENTILATION - HEAT RECOVERY VENTILATION (HRV)



Your house currently has a very old version of a Heat Recovery Exchanger (HRE) unit - a Brock RCF 75. It is a "fanless" unit that was designed to passively draw air into the house and exhaust air out of the use, using pressure differences created by your furnace fan &amp; the x 6" pipes connected from the Heat Recovery Exchanger to the return air plenum of your furnace.



These older "fanless" &amp; passive systems never worked well at all and either left houses overventilated and wasting heat or under-ventilated and creating a poor indoor air quality. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient Heat Recovery Ventilation  (HRV) unit is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



For your house, my software calculations indicate that it is slightly under-ventilated. A new ENERGY STAR® Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit may be what your house requires - one that has its' own circulation fans, control module and booster fan option for when you feel you could use a bit more "fresh air". We highly recommend that homeowners choose high efficiency HRV's (with a minimum 75% heat recovery efficiency) and low electricity use ECM circulation fans.



We discussed a few options that you could explore:

1. Try removing the HRE unit, but keep the 6" fresh air supply pipe as being directly connected from the back wall of the basement straight through to the return air plenum of the furnace &amp; install an "in-line damper" that you can manually adjust from fully closed to fully open. The local Heating contractors will refer to this as a "Fresh Air Makeup Supply". You can operate your bathroom fans on a "timer switch" that will draw some stale air out, while the fresh air supply allows some back in. The Brock HRE should allow this, but who knows how restrictive the heat exchange core is within that unit ?? Also, each time your furnace operates during the winter, it will draw in a bit of fresh air through this pipe. If you find the indoor air is a bit stale with higher humidity, adjust the in-line damper to be more open. If you find the indoor air to be dry with high static electricity, then adjust the damper to be more closed. There are ALOT of houses in Hay River that manage indoor air quality with a fresh air makeup supply pipe.



2. If you find the indoor air could really use some help and you don't want to just exhaust 100% of the interior heat out through bathroom fans .... than I suggest you research replacing the current HRE with an ENERGY STAR® HRV, with a minimum 75% heat recovery efficiency rating, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR® rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, this may increase your energy costs by $ 131 per year and increase your annual energy usage by 2.6 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 189 litre gas hot water tank, that vents up a 3" chimney and has a continuous pilot light.



I have modeled the opportunity to replace the hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95 EF, you could potentially save $ 237 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house currently has an older 80.9% efficient propane furnace that vents up a 4" chimney &amp; it appears to be nearing the time for replacement. I have modeled an option for you to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 96% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 563 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler that is a minimum 97% AFUE. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.



WOOD STOVE HEAT SUPPLEMENT



You have a wood stove in the lower level of your home that is currently used for a high percentage of the heating season, but it depends upon heat naturally rising and mixing throughout the house. We discussed an option for you to have the high efficiency ECM blower fan of the new furnace used to circulate your wood heat. Many HVAC folks can set this up with 2 smart thermostats. The one on the upper floor is set to heating mode to kick on the furnace if the temp gets too low. The one in the basement is set to AC Mode where the set "upper temp" will be triggered by the wood stove heat in the basement ......... turning on only the furnace fan to start circulating that wood stove heat throughout the house !!



Savings:

For now, I have modeled the expected energy savings if you were to use the wood stove to supplement up to 75% of the heating for the lower level of this house (as compared to the new 96% efficient furnace). The calculated net savings (reduced propane gas cost for the furnace, less cost of wood @ the $100/cord self-harvest rate) is about $440 per year (or about $1,000 per year when combined with the savings from the new furnace.



In reality, you are already offsetting a much higher % of your annual heating bill, so my focus in this report is to help you:

1. Upgrade your furnace so you have a new high efficiency one that you can depend on if you need to be away from home and can't tend to the wood stove (and take advantage of our "doubled EEIP rebates this fiscal year)

2. Circulate that great wood heat more effectively throughout the house.



As discussed, you use your Blaze King Princess alot and perhaps you may want to take advantage of our doubled rebates this fiscal year to get a new one (with a dedicated outside combustion air supply). A qualified wood stove is eligible for 50% of the purchase cost up to $ 2,000 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front (SE) facing roof of the second floor, directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 3,746 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,199 and qualify for the full $5,000 from the Canada Greener Homes program.</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood, one bathroom fan (the other 2 were disconnected) &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.9 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current furnace, hot water and wood stove are all chimney vented and can be susceptible to back-drafting. The recommendations in this report allow all three of these appliances to be upgraded to direct vent appliances with dedicated combustion air supplies.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in your home. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - Kitchen range hood

        - 3 x bathroom exhaust fans (but 2 were removed awaiting replacement)

        - Clothes dryer

        - A Fanless, Passive Heat Recovery Exchanger (there is no data available as to its' performance or efficiency)

 

If you choose to replace or install any exhaust fans, please select ENERGY STAR® qualified units. For a new bathroom fans, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $17.77 min, $0.32 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.35 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.32 per litre 

Propane - $0.82 per litre delivered, $3.78 per m3 piped to the house

Wood - $310.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $4.86 per bag, $258.57 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



